<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norman Manley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norman Manley was a Jamaican lawyer and statesman who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party">People's National Party</a> in 1938, after the labour unrest of that year. He led the campaign for self-government and universal suffrage, served as chief minister and then premier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> from 1955 to 1962, and supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> Federation. He is a National Hero of Jamaica, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-manley">Michael Manley</a> later became prime minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norman-manley.jpg" alt="Norman Manley" width="320" height="538" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miami Herald Staff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Norman_Manley,_The_Miami_Herald_1955_08_04_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
